WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:04.120
If you go outside on a clear night
 and look up at the sky,

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:07.960
it looks like the universe
 is beautiful and peaceful,

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:10.200
nothing changes, nothing happens,

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.240
but that really is an illusion -

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.440
the universe is a violent place. 

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:20.400
In the last few decades, 

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:24.320
we've started to monitor asteroids,

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:29.240
there are tens of thousands of big ones
 across the Earth's orbit,

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:33.080
any one of those could hit us.
Just to give some sense

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:36.240
of the energy that these impacts create,

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:42.920
that even small-ish objects,
about ten, twenty meters across,

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.240
when they hit the Earth's atmosphere,

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:50.840
deposit, the energy of twenty, thirty,
fifty Hiroshima bombs.

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:54.480
And it's roughly five-to-ten

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.440
kilometers wide...

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:00.240
An extinction level event. 

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.960
I think that we take our safety

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:07.800
and our existence on this planet
 for granted, so impacts happen,

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:11.880
naturally occurring
 events that can threaten a city,

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:16.760
or a country or a continent,
 or indeed, our civilisation happen.

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:21.760
And the reason we ignore them really, is
 because they don't happen very often - 

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:24.600
but low probability events happen,

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:28.680
and the thing about probabilities
 is that they're not certainties,

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:33.720
so until we see one coming
 and until we can map it's orbit,

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:37.240
then we can't say with any certainty
 when the next one will hit. 

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:40.480
In 'Don't Look Up' the threat is from a comet.

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:48.800
Comets exist in the far frozen 
outer-reaches of the solar system

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:53.120
in a region called the Kuiper Belt
 and then the Oort Cloud

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:54.880
far beyond the orbit of Neptune. 

00:01:54.880 --> 00:02:00.800
And there are millions of these things,
 they're icy snowballs, but they're big.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:05.960
And very often those comets that come into
 the solar system are new discoveries.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:10.120
Now comets don't have orbits

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:13.840
that cross the Earth
 every year, or two years or so -

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:17.440
even if they come back and come back
 and come back, like Halley's comet,

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:22.360
you can be talking about eighty, a hundred, two-hundred, a thousand years,

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.360
and so unlike the asteroids,

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:29.960
we can't really ever assume
 that we can map their orbits -

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:32.440
the film is very accurate, actually.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:38.520
In the film, we have six months from
 detection of this thing to the impact,

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:41.480
and that's fairly typical for a comet.

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:44.640
I keep getting the same result,

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:47.480
a direct hit of Earth in 
six months and fourteen days.

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:50.920
Me too.

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:53.400
So the United States
has an organisation now,

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:58.040
which goes by the catchy
 title of 'The Near-Earth Orbit

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:02.440
Impact Threat Emergency Protocols
 Interagency Working Group',

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:09.280
or 'NEOITEP IWG' I think
 is how they pronounce it.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:14.000
It's a collection of organisations
 that are charged with observing

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:17.040
and mapping the paths
 of potentially dangerous

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:20.320
asteroids, and also then dealing with them

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:24.680
if we see one that may be a threat,
 there is a protocol in place, actually.

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:28.520
There's a flowchart -
 detect it, make precision

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:34.720
observations, assess whether there's a greater than one percent chance of impact-

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:38.840
then you have to model
 what happens if this thing hits,

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:43.200
and eventually there is a civil
 defense contingency,

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:47.760
so do we evacuate the city
 or do we try to move it?

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.760
And a lot of it,
 I suppose, quite disturbingly in a way

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:52.680
but I suppose it's the way it has to be,

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:56.920
a lot of it is to do with the
economic impact of the impact.

00:03:56.920 --> 00:03:59.880
I say we sit tight and assess.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:04.720
The decision you're making
 is to sit tight and assess?

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:07.080
I'm sorry, who is she? 

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:10.520
Because we have powerful computers
 and we have precise observations,

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:14.680
within about, what,
 two months from detection,

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:18.600
then we'll have a complete
 planning scenario,

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:22.320
and on that basis, then we can decide

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:25.600
what we should do about it.
It says this in the report as well,

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:30.000
if we have about three years
 or four years, then

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:33.760
that is how long it takes
 at the moment to construct a spacecraft

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:36.240
which might be able to deflect it.

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:40.440
So, but we need three or four years
 at the moment because we don't have

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:43.760
those spacecraft lined up
 because they're billion dollar spacecraft.

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:48.160
So let's say Chelsea or
Manchester United, spend more

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:50.640
on the wages of the footballers every year

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:55.040
than we spend as a civilisation
 on asteroid defense.

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:58.520
Think about our priorities
 as a civilisation.

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:01.720
Now you might say, 'Well, in that

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:04.800
case, shouldn't we line a load
 of these spacecraft up ready?'

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:11.200
Maybe yes, but the problem is
 we don't know how big that spacecraft

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:14.840
has got to be or what kind of 
technology it's got to employ -

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:18.040
should it have a nuclear warhead on it?
 Should it just nudge it?

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:19.960
Should it just bang into it? 

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:23.560
We don't really know
 until you've seen the sort of object,

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:26.920
and the size of object
 that's causing the problem.

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:31.000
There's a mission called 'DART'
 which is as I speak currently

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:36.080
flying towards an asteroid,
 and it's going to hit the asteroid,

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:39.960
so it's what's called an impactor mission,
 and that's testing out the technologies

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:43.000
that we will need in the future,
 potentially to deflect one.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:45.760
I mean, would you want
 trillions of dollars

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:49.520
of your money spent lining up
 lots of different spacecraft

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:54.120
in order to protect us
 from something that might not happen

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:56.200
in the next ten thousand years? 

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:57.640
Maybe you'd say no,

00:05:57.640 --> 00:05:59.480
and that's the dilemma.

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:01.000
If you're trying to convince

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:03.800
some politicians or a government to invest

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:07.440
hundreds of millions
 or even billions of dollars in technology

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:13.520
and mapping systems and mapping programmes
 to mitigate these events,

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:16.800
then if I say, 'well, it's very,

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:21.200
very unlikely that it'll happen
 when you're in office', right,

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:25.440
then I suppose there's a tendency to go 'oh
 well, leave it for the next person then',

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:28.640
and that's just the way that our brains
 work and our political cycles work.

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:33.840
One of the things I love about the film,
 and I think the reasons that it's

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:36.880
very important, is that it really does deal

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:40.520
with the level of trust

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:43.880
that people have in scientific statements.

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:46.560
You see it with Leo DiCaprio's character,

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:49.680
I think it's beautifully acted
 and beautifully written, where

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:51.600
he tries to be very very precise -

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:55.520
- exactly ninety nine point seven eight 
percent to be exact

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:57.680
-Oh! Great! OK, so it's not a hundred percent.

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:00.640
Well, scientists
 never like to say one hundred percent. 

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:03.360
Call it seventy percent 
and let's just let's move on. 

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:07.880
-and suddenly there's a window
 to sort of distrust the science and

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:13.240
deflect yourself away from the potential
 catastrophic nature of the prediction.

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:16.520
We know that there are objects out there

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:20.800
that we have not yet
 discovered, that may pose a threat.

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:23.520
And that's notoriously difficult,

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:27.240
you see it in the film.
 In fact, in the film, you see that

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:31.400
people can see the thing
 in the sky, right,

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:34.520
you can actually look up and see it,

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:40.640
and still there's a robust public debate
 about whether it's there...

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:44.400
We're hearing that
 there is no comet -

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:48.520
...even though you can look up and see it. 

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:51.360
We, we've been trying to tell you!

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:54.320
We've been trying to tell you 
this whole time, it's right there,

00:07:55.640 --> 00:07:56.720
it's right there, Kate.

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:00.480
 I think one of the problems we face as a 
civilisation

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:03.720
is we don't know how valuable we are.

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:05.640
 In our galaxy, the Milky Way, 

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:10.760
there are four hundred billion stars,
 and countless trillions of planets,

00:08:10.760 --> 00:08:13.480
and yet it is possible
 that you could count

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:16.800
the number of civilisations
 on the fingers of one hand.

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:22.320
In fact, it's possible that
 there is only one, and that's us, here.

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:26.240
Talk about meaning
 there's a word that doesn't sound

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:27.960
particularly scientific, 'meaning', 

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:30.240
what does it mean to exist? 

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:34.720
Well I would argue that meaning
 is a property of intelligence,

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:39.560
and if this planet is the only place
 where intelligence exists,

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:41.560
then I would argue
 that this is the only place

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:45.440
where meaning exists
 in a galaxy of four hundred billion stars.

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:50.880
And it's possible that by not taking
 the violence of the universe seriously,

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:54.240
we may allow or be complicit in,

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:57.440
the destruction of meaning in a galaxy.

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:03.760
There's a very famous name in space
 exploration, Robert Zubrin.

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:06.560
I interviewed him once and he said to me

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:11.640
'ideas have consequences,
 and the worst idea that we have ever

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:16.880
had as a human civilisation is that
 we have access to limited resources'.

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:21.840
The correct thing to say is we have access
 to limited resources on Earth

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:24.240
and indeed we have to be extremely careful

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:27.160
because there are limited
 resources on Earth,

00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:31.360
that means that we fight over them and
 we damage our planet and our environment,

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:35.000
but, Zubrin points out, the idea is false.

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:39.720
It's false because the moment you lift
 your gaze up from the surface of the Earth

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:43.760
and out into space
 to the asteroid belt and beyond,

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:47.360
then you see that there are effectively
 unlimited resources out there.

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:50.320
Ladies and gentlemen,
 what we thought, what the world

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:54.400
thought was an impending
 and terrifying danger

00:09:55.640 --> 00:09:58.240
turns out to be an
 astonishing opportunity,

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:04.360
There's one fact that I love,
 which is out there in the asteroid belt

00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:08.680
which is where most of the asteroids are,
 there is enough metal

00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:15.200
to build a skyscraper, a hundred stories
 high and cover the entire Earth in it.

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:19.440
Asteroids are not just a threat,
 they're a tremendous opportunity,

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:20.880
You see that in the film,

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:24.360
in fact the fact
 that they are potentially

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:27.200
tremendously valuable
 ultimately leads to our destruction.

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:31.880
The comet actually contains
almost one hundred and forty

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:34.040
trillion dollars worth of assets.

00:10:34.040 --> 00:10:35.080
about forty trilly -

00:10:35.080 --> 00:10:36.560
In terms of mining asteroids, 

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:38.160
we know what many of 

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:40.720
the asteroids are made of
 because a lot them have landed on Earth,

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:43.040
I mean, here's a piece of one,
 and it's very heavy.

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:45.480
Many of them are metal rich

00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:48.720
and so these are valuable things.

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:55.040
Interestingly the technology that we need
 to deflect them to mitigate the risks,

00:10:55.040 --> 00:10:58.200
is pretty much the same technology
 that we need to go and mine them -

00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:03.080
so the question arises, what role do
 big corporations currently play?

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:07.560
In the film, the bad guys, well virtually
 everyone's a bad guy actually,

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:10.920
but one of the primary bad guys
 is Bash Cellular.

00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:14.080
I don't think we should
 demonise the companies

00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:18.680
that build the technology,
 because one day we're going to need them.

00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:21.240
I call this one Free-mo the first - 

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:24.800
If you look at what 

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:29.240
SpaceX have done and
Blue Origin are doing,

00:11:29.240 --> 00:11:34.720
then they are making access
 to space reliable and relatively cheap.

00:11:34.720 --> 00:11:36.920
And the revolution in the last decade

00:11:36.920 --> 00:11:40.880
or so has been the invention
 of reusable rockets.

00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:47.280
Imagine how expensive
 it would be to fly from London to New York

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:50.720
if you got on the plane,
 flew across the Atlantic,

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:53.560
got off the plane
 and then the plane exploded,

00:11:53.560 --> 00:11:54.440
it would be ridiculous -

00:11:54.440 --> 00:11:56.240
single use aircraft. 

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:03.040
One of the things
 I really love about the film

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:08.320
is that it addresses a problem
 that worries I think all of us in science,

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:13.280
and all of us in many fields of human
 endeavor actually, which is how do we

00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:16.720
compete with all the noise
 in our civilisation?

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:22.080
How do we make people pay attention
 to reliable and important knowledge

00:12:22.400 --> 00:12:26.120
when there's so much unreliable
 and trivial knowledge out there

00:12:26.120 --> 00:12:28.280
competing for mental space? 

00:12:28.280 --> 00:12:29.880
Just think about it for a moment,

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:34.320
are you really more interested
 in the private life

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:39.440
of your favourite celebrity
 than you are in the protection

00:12:39.440 --> 00:12:43.440
of intelligent life in a galaxy 
of four hundred billion stars?

00:12:43.440 --> 00:12:45.680
Are you really more concerned about that?

00:12:48.200 --> 00:12:51.920
Probably, you'd probably say
 yeah, you're probably saying, 'yes'. 

00:12:51.920 --> 00:12:57.800
Actually, probably as I speak, you're on your phone trying to have a look at, what,

00:12:57.800 --> 00:12:59.760
you know, what the football results are.

00:13:02.400 --> 00:13:04.480
Thing of it is, we really,

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:08.760
we really did have everything didn't we? 

00:13:11.560 --> 00:13:13.600
I mean when you think about it. 

00:13:15.480 --> 00:13:18.320
The last line that Leo DiCaprio's
 character delivers,

00:13:18.320 --> 00:13:23.600
I think is really powerful
 and important line, he's right

00:13:24.160 --> 00:13:26.320
we do have it all. 

00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:30.640
This is the only place where anything
probably matters.

00:13:30.640 --> 00:13:33.040
I think it would be a tragedy,

00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:36.840
I'm sure you agree
 if you only realise that

00:13:36.840 --> 00:13:41.240
with thirty seconds to go
 before the comet hits.

00:13:41.240 --> 00:13:45.440
My advice, if we're to avoid the fate

00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:48.360
of our civilisation depicted in the film,

00:13:48.360 --> 00:13:54.200
is to look up. To face nature
 in all its beauty and all its terror.

00:13:54.200 --> 00:13:57.200
Don't hide your head in the sand,

00:13:57.200 --> 00:13:59.680
don't shy away from the universe beyond

00:13:59.680 --> 00:14:04.000
because if you do that, then
 before we know it,

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:07.520
something will come tearing down from
 the skies and wipe us out.

